Implementation Plan — User Service
Problem Statement: Build the user-service as a standalone Spring Boot 3 / Maven microservice that handles JWT-based authentication, user profile management, and address CRUD. It is the first business service in the e-commerce platform and the source of JWTs consumed by every other service.

Requirements:

Standalone Maven project at Ecomm POC/user-service/
JWT via HS256 using JWT_SECRET env var (shared with gateway)
Access token: 15 min TTL, Refresh token: 7 day TTL
Refresh tokens stored hashed in PostgreSQL refresh_tokens table; blacklisted JTIs on logout stored in Redis
Redis cache for user profiles (user:{id}, TTL 15 min)
No Kafka / outbox in this iteration
Standard error envelope {timestamp, status, error, message, path, traceId} on all errors
DTOs always separate from JPA entities (MapStruct)
Internal Feign endpoint GET /users/{userId} (called by Order Service later)
Registers with Eureka on startup
Runs inside ecommerce-net Docker network per the existing docker-compose.yml



Task Breakdown:

Task 1: Project scaffold & dependencies

Create user-service/ directory with a pom.xml declaring: spring-boot-starter-web, spring-boot-starter-security, spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, spring-boot-starter-data-redis, spring-boot-starter-validation, spring-boot-starter-actuator, spring-cloud-starter-netflix-eureka-client, jjwt-api + jjwt-impl + jjwt-jackson (0.12.x), mapstruct, lombok, postgresql, springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui
Add UserServiceApplication.java with @SpringBootApplication
Add application.yml wiring up datasource (pointing to user_db), Redis, Eureka, and a jwt.secret / jwt.access-token-expiry / jwt.refresh-token-expiry config block
Add Dockerfile (multi-stage: Maven build → JRE 17 runtime image)
Test: mvn compile passes cleanly; application starts and registers with Eureka (manual smoke test with docker-compose infra up)
Demo: mvn compile succeeds with zero errors; Spring Boot banner appears on startup
Task 2: JPA entities & repositories

Create the four entities mapped to user_db tables: User, Role, Address, RefreshToken — annotations matching the SQL schema (@Table, @Column, UUID PKs via @GeneratedValue(strategy = UUID), @ManyToMany for user_roles, @OneToMany for addresses and refresh tokens)
Create repositories: UserRepository (findByEmail), RoleRepository (findByName), AddressRepository (findByUserIdOrderByIsDefaultDesc), RefreshTokenRepository (findByTokenHash, deleteByUserId)
Test: @DataJpaTest + Testcontainers Postgres — verify UserRepository.findByEmail, unique email constraint, AddressRepository.findByUserId, RefreshTokenRepository.findByTokenHash
Demo: Repository tests pass; a User with a Role and an Address can be saved and retrieved correctly
Task 3: DTOs, mappers, and exception infrastructure

Create all request DTOs with Bean Validation: RegisterRequest (@Email, @NotBlank, @Size(min=8) on password), LoginRequest, RefreshRequest, UpdateUserRequest, AddressRequest
Create all response DTOs: UserResponse, TokenResponse, AddressResponse, ErrorResponse
Implement UserMapper and AddressMapper with MapStruct (@Mapper(componentModel = "spring"))
Create the exception hierarchy: AppException(HttpStatus, String) → ResourceNotFoundException, ConflictException, UnauthorizedException
Implement GlobalExceptionHandler (@RestControllerAdvice) handling AppException, MethodArgumentNotValidException, and generic Exception → always emits the standard ErrorResponse JSON shape including traceId from MDC
Test: Unit-test mappers (entity → DTO round-trip); unit-test GlobalExceptionHandler for each exception type
Demo: Mapping tests pass; throwing a ResourceNotFoundException from a controller produces the correct error JSON
Task 4: JWT service

Implement JwtService encapsulating all token logic:
generateAccessToken(User) — builds a JWT with claims sub=userId, roles, jti=uuid, exp=now+15m, signed with HS256 using the configured secret
generateRefreshToken() — returns a cryptographically random opaque string (UUID or SecureRandom)
validateAccessToken(String) → parsed Claims or throws UnauthorizedException
extractUserId(Claims), extractJti(Claims), extractRoles(Claims)
Implement RedisConfig and JwtFilter (extends OncePerRequestFilter): reads Authorization: Bearer, validates the token, checks the Redis blacklist (blacklist:{jti}), builds a UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken from X-User-Id / X-User-Roles (for future gateway-forwarded requests) or directly from the validated JWT
Test: Unit-test JwtService: generate → validate round-trip, expired token throws, tampered token throws
Demo: JwtService tests pass; a generated access token can be decoded and round-trips correctly
Task 5: Auth endpoints — register & login

Implement AuthService.register(): check email uniqueness (throw ConflictException if duplicate), hash password with BCrypt, persist User + assign ROLE_USER, generate access + refresh tokens, persist hashed refresh token in refresh_tokens table, return TokenResponse
Implement AuthService.login(): load user by email, verify BCrypt password (throw UnauthorizedException on mismatch), generate new token pair, persist refresh token, return TokenResponse
Implement AuthController with POST /auth/register (201) and POST /auth/login (200), both marked public in SecurityConfig (permit all on /auth/**)
Configure SecurityConfig: stateless sessions, disable CSRF, permit /auth/** and /actuator/**, require authentication everywhere else
Test: @SpringBootTest + Testcontainers Postgres + Redis — full HTTP flow: register → 201 with valid JWT; duplicate register → 409; login with wrong password → 401
Demo: curl POST /auth/register returns a 201 with accessToken and refreshToken; decoding the JWT shows correct sub and roles
Task 6: Auth endpoints — refresh & logout

Implement AuthService.refresh(): look up non-revoked RefreshToken by hash (throw UnauthorizedException if not found/expired/revoked), load user, issue new token pair, rotate refresh token (revoke old row, insert new), return TokenResponse
Implement AuthService.logout(): extract jti from the current access token, write blacklist:{jti} to Redis with TTL = remaining token life, revoke the RefreshToken row for this user
Add POST /auth/refresh (200, public) and POST /auth/logout (204, requires User auth) to AuthController
Test: Integration tests — refresh happy path; refresh with revoked token → 401; logout then use old access token → 401 (Redis blacklist hit)
Demo: Full auth lifecycle works end-to-end: register → login → refresh → logout → old token rejected
Task 7: User profile & address endpoints

Implement UserService: getMe(userId) with Redis cache-aside (user:{id}, TTL 15 min), updateMe(userId, request) (update name/phone, evict cache), getAddresses(userId), addAddress(userId, request) (enforce single default: if isDefault=true, clear existing defaults first), updateAddress(userId, addressId, request), deleteAddress(userId, addressId)
Implement UserController with all endpoints from the API contract: GET /users/me, PUT /users/me, GET /users/me/addresses, POST /users/me/addresses, PUT /users/me/addresses/{id}, DELETE /users/me/addresses/{id}
Add the internal GET /users/{userId} endpoint (no auth filter needed if called by Feign within the Docker network — permit in SecurityConfig or protect with a header check)
Extract userId from the Authentication object (set by JwtFilter)
Test: Integration tests for each endpoint — GET /users/me returns profile; PUT /users/me updates and evicts cache; address add/update/delete lifecycle; GET /users/{userId} returns correct profile
Demo: Full user and address CRUD works; Redis profile cache verified by hitting GET /users/me twice and confirming the second call is served from cache (DB query count = 1)
Task 8: OpenAPI docs & Docker wiring

Ensure springdoc-openapi is configured: springdoc.api-docs.path=/v3/api-docs, all controller methods have @Operation and @ApiResponse annotations
Verify the Dockerfile builds a runnable image and the user-service block in docker-compose.yml starts the service correctly against the existing infra
Smoke-test the full docker-compose stack: postgres + redis + kafka + eureka + user-service all healthy
Test: docker compose up user-service → service registers in Eureka dashboard (http://localhost:8761); GET /v3/api-docs returns the OpenAPI spec; all previous integration tests still pass
Demo: Eureka dashboard shows USER-SERVICE as UP; Swagger UI at http://localhost:8080/swagger-ui.html (via gateway) lists all user-service endpoints




